# Resources

Resources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit
CRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under
`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent
element-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.

## Resource Types

| Type | Purpose | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |
| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |

## Commands

```bash
# Create a resource
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Architecture design")'
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Research findings")'

# Replace an existing resource
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="revised findings", description="Research findings v2")'
task-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome

# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it
task-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description "Recovered result"

# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'
task-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm

# View resources on an element
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'
```

## Element-Owned Storage

Resources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each
element gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is
stored below it:

```
.task-board/.resources/
├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/
│   ├── findings/
│   │   └── investigation.md
│   ├── artifacts/
│   │   └── run-1/
│   │       └── output.json
│   ├── reports/
│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md
│   └── revisions/
│       └── 2/
│           └── review.md
└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/
    └── research.md         # outcome
```

The resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose
the hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every
segment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an
absolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are
the only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly
encoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same
owning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write
payloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.

Directories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and
handoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report
cannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.

Local and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and
crash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged
element delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion
currently fails before mutation with the typed
`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD
remains available.

References are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:
```markdown
## Precondition Resources
- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design

## Outcome Resources
- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings
```

## Spawn Integration

Precondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:

1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task
2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically
3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions

## Best Practices

- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning
- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes
- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically
- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload
- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists
- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate
- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage
- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence
- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually
- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it
- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts

